{"type":"message","id":"a5db6d82","parentId":"e100d8b2","timestamp":"2026-01-30T21:14:36.024Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are working on the HHC Brooklyn Dental Bid Takeoff project at /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/\n\nYour job: Refactor the bid takeoff workflow into a multi-agent \"Team of Rivals\" architecture using Clawdbot's sessions_spawn capability.\n\nContext:\n- We have 568 bid line items (298 mapped so far), 86 drawing sheets, 1,058-page spec\n- Currently single-agent approach — one AI does everything\n- Known issues: GWB partition layer discrepancy, fire-rated door missing from bid\n- Paper reference: arxiv.org/abs/2601.14351 — specialized agent teams with opposing incentives\n\nCreate a file at /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md that includes:\n\n1. **Agent Roles** — Define each agent (Planner, Executor, Critic, Domain Expert) with exact responsibilities and prompt templates\n2. **Workflow Pipeline** — Step-by-step: how a bid line item flows from raw drawing → quantity → priced item → QA-checked output\n3. **Adversarial Checks** — Specific checks the Critic agent runs (cross-ref specs vs drawings, unit consistency, missing items, code compliance)\n4. **Error Interception Rules** — What triggers a flag vs auto-resolve vs escalate to Doug\n5. **Implementation Plan** — How to wire this up using sessions_spawn with specific task prompts for each agent role\n6. **Data Hygiene** — How to keep context clean (summaries only, no raw PDF dumps into agent context)\n\nAlso review the existing files in that project directory to understand current state before designing the architecture.\n\nBe thorough — this becomes our production workflow."}],"timestamp":1769807676023}}
