{"type":"message","id":"2ae93567","parentId":"24ca8e5a","timestamp":"2026-01-30T21:14:48.544Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_01LD9fw1miu35y1sVTf2oETw","toolName":"exec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Type: list, len: 21\n[\n  {\n    \"section\": \"024119\",\n    \"title\": \"SELECTIVE DEMOLITION\",\n    \"division\": \"02\",\n    \"start_page\": 72,\n    \"manufacturers\": [],\n    \"products\": [],\n    \"text_length\": 8387,\n    \"full_text\": \"South Brooklyn Health \\n5th Fl Dental Suite \\nBID SET \\nSECTION 024119 -\\nSELECTIVE DEMOLITION\\nCopyright \\u00a9 2024 by the American Institute of Architects. Warning: This AIA MasterSpec-based document is protected by U.S. Copyright Law and International\\nTreaties. It was created by \\\"Mancini Duffy\\\" for \\\"South Brooklyn Health 5th Fl Dental Suite\\\". A valid, current MasterSpec license is required for editing and use of this \\ndocument for any other project.(25268)\\n024119 - 1\\nSECTION 024119 - SELECTIVE DEMOLITION \\nPART 1 - GENERAL \\n1.1 \\nSUMMARY \\nA.\\nSection Includes:\\n1.\\nDemolition and removal of selected portions of building or structure.\\n2.\\nSalvage of existing items to be reused or recycled.\\n1.2 \\nMATERIALS OWNERSHIP \\nA.\\nUnless otherwise indicated, demolition waste becomes property of Contractor.\\n1.3\\nINFORMATIONAL SUBMITTALS \\n1.4\\nCLOSEOUT SUBMITTALS \\n1.5\\nQUALITY ASSURANCE \\n1.6\\nFIELD CONDITIONS \\nA.\\nOwner will occupy portions of building immediately adjacent to selective\\nConduct selective demolition so Owner's operations will not be disrupted.\\ndemolition area.\\nB.\\nNotify Architect and Owner of discrepancies between existing conditions and Drawings before\\nproceeding with selective demolition.\\nC.\\nStorage or sale of removed items or materials on-site is not permitted.\\nD.\\nArrange selective demolition schedule so as not to interfere with Owner's operations.\\nPART 2 - PRODUCTS \\n2.1 \\nPERFORMANCE REQUIREMENTS \\nA. \\nRegulatory Requirements: Comply with governing EPA notification regulations before beginning\\nselective demolition. Comply with hauling and disposal regulations of authorities having\\njurisdiction.\\nB. \\nStandards: Comply with ANSI/ASSP A10.6 and NFPA 241.\\n\\nSouth Brooklyn Health \\n5th Fl Dental Suite \\nBID SET \\nSECTION 024119 -\\nSELECTIVE DEMOLITION\\nCopyright \\u00a9 2024 by the American Institute of Architects. Warning: This AIA MasterSpec-based document is protected by U.S. Copyright Law and International\\nTreaties. It was created by \\\"Mancini Duffy\\\" for \\\"South Brooklyn Health 5th Fl Dental Suite\\\". A valid, current MasterSpec license is required for editing and use of this \\ndocument for any other project.(25268)\\n024119 - 2\\nPART 3 - EXECUTION \\n3.1 \\nEXAMINATION \\nA.\\nVerify that utilities have been disconnected and capped before starting selective demolition\\noperations.\\nB.\\nInventory and record the condition of items to be removed and salvaged.\\n3.2 \\nPREPARATION \\n3.3 \\nUTILITY SERVICES AND MECHANICAL/ELECTRICAL SYSTEMS \\nA.\\nExisting Services/Systems to Be Removed, Relocated, or Abandoned: Locate, identify,\\ndisconnect, and seal or cap off utility services and mechanical/electrical systems serving areas to\\nbe selectively demolished.\\n1.\\nArrange with Owner to shut off utilities with utility companies.\\n2.\\nIf services/systems are required to be removed, relocated, or abandoned, provide temporary\\nservices/systems that bypass area of selective demolition and that maintain continuity of\\nservices/systems to other parts of building.\\n3.\\nDisconnect, demolish, and remove fire-suppression systems, plumbing, and HVAC\\nsystems, equipment, and components indicated on Drawings to be removed.\\na.\\nPiping to Be Removed: Remove portion of piping indicated to be removed and cap\\nor plug remaining piping with same or compatible piping material.\\nb.\\nPiping to Be Abandoned in Place: Drain piping and cap or plug piping with same or\\ncompatible piping material and leave in place.\\nc.\\nEquipment to Be Removed: Disconnect and cap services and remove equipment.\\nd.\\nEquipment to Be Removed and Reinstalled: Disconnect and cap services and\\nremove, clean, and store equipment; when appropriate, reinstall, reconnect, and\\nmake equipment operational.\\ne.\\nEquipment to Be Removed and Salvaged: Disconnect and cap services and remove\\nequipment and deliver to Owner.\\nf.\\nDucts to Be Removed: Remove portion of ducts indicated to be removed and plug\\nremaining ducts with same or compatible ductwork material.\\ng.\\nDucts to Be Abandoned in Place: Cap or plug ducts with same or compatible\\nductwork material and leave in place.\\n3.4 \\nPROTECTION \\nA.\\nRemove temporary barricades and protections where hazards no longer exist.\\n\\nSouth Brooklyn Health \\n5th Fl Dental Suite \\nBID SET \\nSECTION 024119 -\\nSELECTIVE DEMOLITION\\nCopyright \\u00a9 2024 by the American Institute of Architects. Warning: This AIA MasterSpec-based document is protected by U.S. Copyright Law and International\\nTreaties. It was created by \\\"Mancini Duffy\\\" for \\\"South Brooklyn Health 5th Fl Dental Suite\\\". A valid, current MasterSpec license is required for editing and use of this \\ndocument for any other project.(25268)\\n024119 - 3\\n \\n \\n \\n3.5 \\nSELECTIVE DEMOLITION \\n \\nA. \\nGeneral: Demolish and remove existing construction only to the extent required by new \\nconstruction and as indicated. Use methods required to complete the Work within limitations of \\ngoverning regulations and as follows: \\n \\n1. \\nNeatly cut openings and holes plumb, square, and true to dimensions required. Use cutting \\nmethods least likely to damage construction to remain or adjoining construction. Use hand \\ntools or small power tools designed for sawing or grinding, not hammering and chopping. \\nTemporarily cover openings to remain. \\n2. \\nCut or drill from the exposed or finished side into concealed surfaces to avoid marring \\nexisting finished surfaces. \\n3. \\nDo not use cutting torches until work area is cleared of flammable materials. At concealed \\nspaces, such as duct and pipe interiors, verify condition and contents of hidden space before \\nstarting flame-cutting operations. Maintain portable fire-suppression devices during flame-\\ncutting operations. \\n4. \\nMaintain fire watch during and for as required hours after flame-cutting operations, if any. \\n5. \\nLocate selective demolition equipment and remove debris and materials so as not to impose \\nexcessive loads on supporting walls, floors, or framing. \\n6. \\nDispose of demolished items and materials promptly. Comply with requirements in Section \\n017419 \\\"Construction Waste Management and Disposal.\\\" \\n \\nB. \\nRemoved and Salvaged Items: \\n \\n1. \\nClean salvaged items. \\n2. \\nStore items in a secure area until delivery to Owner. \\n3. \\nProtect items from damage during transport and storage. \\n \\nC. \\nRemoved and Reinstalled Items: \\n \\n1. \\nClean and repair items to functional condition adequate for intended reuse. \\n2. \\nPack or crate items after cleaning and repairing. Identify contents of containers. \\n3. \\nProtect items from damage during transport and storage. \\n4. \\nReinstall items in locations indicated. Comply with installation requirements for new \\nmaterials and equipment. Provide connections, supports, and miscellaneous materials \\nnecessary to make item functional for use indicated. \\n \\nD. \\nExisting Items to Remain: Protect construction indicated to remain against damage and soiling \\nduring selective demolition. Clean and reinstall in their original locations after selective \\ndemolition operations are complete. \\n \\n3.6 \\nCLEANING \\n \\nA. \\nRemove demolition waste materials from Project site and dispose of them in an EPA-approved \\nconstruction and demolition waste landfill acceptable to authorities having jurisdiction. and \\nrecycle or dispose of them according to Section 017419 \\\"Construction Waste Management and \\nDisposal.\\\" \\n \\n1. \\nDo not allow demolished materials to accumulate on-site. \\n\\nSouth Brooklyn Health \\n5th Fl Dental Suite \\nBID SET \\nSECTION 024119 -\\nSELECTIVE DEMOLITION\\nCopyright \\u00a9 2024 by the American Institute of Architects. Warning: This AIA MasterSpec-based document is protected by U.S. Copyright Law and International\\nTreaties. It was created by \\\"Mancini Duffy\\\" for \\\"South Brooklyn Health 5th Fl Dental Suite\\\". A valid, current MasterSpec license is required for editing and use of this \\ndocument for any other project.(25268)\\n024119 - 4\\n2.\\nRemove and transport debris in a manner that will prevent spillage on adjacent surfaces\\nand areas.\\n3.\\nRemove debris from elevated portions of building by chute, hoist, or other device that will\\nconvey debris to grade level in a controlled descent. Coordinate with Building\\nMa\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"section\": \"061000\",\n    \"title\": \"ROUGH\",\n    \"division\": \"06\",\n    \"start_page\": 81,\n    \"manufacturers\": [],\n    \"products\": [\n      \"1.\",\n      \"Treated materials are to have a moisture content of 28 percent or less when\",\n      \"Install engineered wood products to comply with\"\n    ],\n    \"text_length\": 8113,\n    \"full_text\": \"South Brooklyn Health \\n5th Fl Dental Suite \\nBID SET \\nSECTION 061000 - ROUGH\\nCARPENTRY\\nCopyright \\u00a9 2024 by the American Institute of Architects. Warning: This AIA MasterSpec-based document is protected by U.S. Copyright Law and International\\nTreaties. It was created by \\\"Mancini Duffy\\\" for \\\"South Brooklyn Health 5th Fl Dental Suite\\\". A valid, current MasterSpec license is required for editing and use of this \\ndocument for any other project.(25268)\\n061000 - 1\\nSECTION 061000 - ROUGH CARPENTRY \\nPART 1 - GENERAL \\n1.1 \\nSUMMARY \\nA.\\nSection Includes:\\n1.\\nWood blocking and nailers.\\n2.\\nWood sleepers.\\n3.\\nPlywood backing panels.\\n1.2 \\nACTION SUBMITTALS \\nA.\\nProduct Data:\\n1.\\nFor each type of process and factory-fabricated product.\\n1.3 \\nINFORMATIONAL SUBMITTALS \\n1.4 \\nQUALITY ASSURANCE \\nPART 2 - PRODUCTS \\n2.1 \\nWOOD PRODUCTS, GENERAL \\nA. \\nLumber: Comply with DOC PS 20 and applicable rules of grading agencies indicated. If no\\ngrading agency is indicated, comply with the applicable rules of any rules-writing agency certified\\nby the ALSC Board of Review. Grade lumber by an agency certified by the ALSC Board of\\nReview to inspect and grade lumber under the rules indicated.\\n1.\\nFactory mark each piece of lumber with grade stamp of grading agency.\\n2.\\nFor exposed lumber indicated to receive a stained or natural finish, mark grade stamp on\\nend or back of each piece .\\n3.\\nDress lumber, S4S, unless otherwise indicated.\\nB. \\nMaximum Moisture Content:\\n1.\\nBoards: 19 percent unless otherwise indicated.\\n\\nSouth Brooklyn Health \\n5th Fl Dental Suite \\nBID SET \\nSECTION 061000 - ROUGH\\nCARPENTRY\\nCopyright \\u00a9 2024 by the American Institute of Architects. Warning: This AIA MasterSpec-based document is protected by U.S. Copyright Law and International\\nTreaties. It was created by \\\"Mancini Duffy\\\" for \\\"South Brooklyn Health 5th Fl Dental Suite\\\". A valid, current MasterSpec license is required for editing and use of this \\ndocument for any other project.(25268)\\n061000 - 2\\n2.2 \\nFIRE-RETARDANT-TREATMENT \\nA. \\nGeneral: Where fire-retardant-treated materials are indicated, materials are to comply with\\nrequirements in this article, that are acceptable to authorities having jurisdiction, and with fire- \\ntest-response characteristics specified as determined by testing identical products per test method\\nindicated by a qualified testing agency.\\nB. \\nFire-Retardant-Treated Lumber and Plywood by Pressure Process: Products with a flame-spread\\nindex of 25 or less when tested according to ASTM E84, and with no evidence of significant\\nprogressive combustion when the test is extended an additional 20 minutes, and with the flame\\nfront not extending more than 10.5 feet beyond the centerline of the burners at any time during\\nthe test.\\n1.\\nInterior Type A: Treated materials are to have a moisture content of 28 percent or less when\\ntested according to ASTM D3201/D3201M at 92 percent relative humidity. Use where\\nexterior type is not indicated.\\nC. \\nKiln-dry lumber after treatment to maximum moisture content of 19 percent. Kiln-dry plywood\\nafter treatment to maximum moisture content of 15 percent.\\nD. \\nIdentify fire-retardant-treated wood with appropriate classification marking of qualified testing\\nagency.\\nE. \\nE. Application: Treat all rough carpentry unless otherwise indicated\\n2.3 \\nPLYWOOD BACKING PANELS \\nA. \\nEquipment Backing Panels: Plywood, DOC PS 1, , fire-retardant treated, in thickness indicated\\nor, if not indicated, not less than 3/4-inch nominal thickness.\\n2.4 \\nFASTENERS \\nA. \\nGeneral: Fasteners are to be of size and type indicated and comply with requirements specified in\\nthis article for material and manufacture. Provide nails or screws, in sufficient length, to penetrate\\nnot less than 1-1/2 inches into wood substrate.\\n1.\\nWhere rough carpentry is exposed to weather, in ground contact, pressure-preservative\\ntreated, or in area of high relative humidity, provide fasteners with hot-dip zinc coating\\ncomplying with ASTM A153/A153M or of Type 304 stainless steel.\\nB. \\nPower-Driven Fasteners: Fastener systems with an evaluation report acceptable to authorities\\nhaving jurisdiction, based on ICC-ES AC70.\\n2.5 \\nMETAL FRAMING ANCHORS \\nA. \\nAllowable design loads, as published by manufacturer, are to meet or exceed those of products of\\nmanufacturers listed. Manufacturer's published values are to be determined from empirical data\\nor by rational engineering analysis and demonstrated by comprehensive testing performed\\n\\nSouth Brooklyn Health \\n5th Fl Dental Suite \\nBID SET \\nSECTION 061000 - ROUGH\\nCARPENTRY\\nCopyright \\u00a9 2024 by the American Institute of Architects. Warning: This AIA MasterSpec-based document is protected by U.S. Copyright Law and International\\nTreaties. It was created by \\\"Mancini Duffy\\\" for \\\"South Brooklyn Health 5th Fl Dental Suite\\\". A valid, current MasterSpec license is required for editing and use of this \\ndocument for any other project.(25268)\\n061000 - 3\\n \\n \\n \\nby a qualified independent testing agency. Framing anchors are to be punched for fasteners \\nadequate to withstand same loads as framing anchors. \\n \\nB. \\nGalvanized-Steel Sheet: Hot-dip, zinc-coated steel sheet complying with ASTM A653/A653M, \\nG60 coating designation. \\n \\n1. \\nUse for interior locations unless otherwise indicated. \\n \\nC. \\nHot-Dip, Heavy-Galvanized Steel Sheet: ASTM A653/A653M; structural steel (SS), high- \\nstrength low-alloy steel Type A (HSLAS Type A), or high-strength low-alloy steel Type B \\n(HSLAS Type B); G185 coating designation; and not less than 0.036 inch thick. \\n \\n1. \\nUse for wood-preservative-treated lumber and where indicated. \\n \\nPART 3 - EXECUTION \\n \\n3.1 \\nINSTALLATION \\n \\nA. \\nFraming Standard: Comply with AF&PA's WCD 1, \\\"Details for Conventional Wood Frame \\nConstruction,\\\" unless otherwise indicated. \\n \\nB. \\nFraming with Engineered Wood Products: Install engineered wood products to comply with \\nmanufacturer's written instructions. \\n \\nC. \\nSet work to required levels and lines, with members plumb, true to line, cut, and fitted. Fit rough \\ncarpentry accurately to other construction. Locate furring, nailers, blocking, and similar supports \\nto comply with requirements for attaching other construction. \\n \\nD. \\nInstall shear wall panels to comply with manufacturer's written instructions. \\n \\nE. \\nInstall metal framing anchors to comply with manufacturer's written instructions. Install fasteners \\nthrough each fastener hole. \\n \\nF. \\nDo not splice structural members between supports unless otherwise indicated. \\n \\nG. \\nComply with AWPA M4 for applying field treatment to cut surfaces of preservative-treated \\nlumber. \\n \\nH. \\nWhere wood-preservative-treated lumber is installed adjacent to metal decking, install continuous \\nflexible flashing separator between wood and metal decking. \\n \\nI. \\nSecurely attach rough carpentry work to substrate by anchoring and fastening as indicated, \\ncomplying with the following: \\n \\n1. \\nTable 2304.9.1, \\\"Fastening Schedule,\\\" in ICC's International Building Code (IBC). \\n2. \\nTable R602.3(1), \\\"Fastener Schedule for Structural Members,\\\" and Table R602.3(2), \\n\\\"Alternate Attachments,\\\" in ICC's International Residential Code for One- and Two- \\nFamily Dwellings. \\n3. \\nICC-ES evaluation report for fastener. \\n\\nSouth Brooklyn Health \\n5th Fl Dental Suite \\nBID SET \\nSECTION 061000 - ROUGH\\nCARPENTRY\\nCopyright \\u00a9 2024 by the American Institute of Architects. Warning: This AIA MasterSpec-based document is protected by U.S. Copyright Law and International\\nTreaties. It was created by \\\"Mancini Duffy\\\" for \\\"South Brooklyn Health 5th Fl Dental Suite\\\". A valid, current MasterSpec license is required for editing and use of this \\ndocument for any other project.(25268)\\n061000 - 4\\n3.2 \\nPROTECTION \\nA. \\nProtect wood that has been treated with inorganic boron (SBX) from weather. If, despite\\nprotection, inorganic boron-treated wood becomes wet, apply EPA-registered borate treatment.\\nApply borate solution by spraying to comply with EPA-registered label.\\nB. \\nProtect rough carpentry from weather. If, despite protection, rough carpentry becomes wet , apply\\nEPA-registered bo\"\n  }\n]"}],"details":{"status":"completed","exitCode":0,"durationMs":42,"aggregated":"Type: list, len: 21\n[\n  {\n    \"section\": \"024119\",\n    \"title\": \"SELECTIVE DEMOLITION\",\n    \"division\": \"02\",\n    \"start_page\": 72,\n    \"manufacturers\": [],\n    \"products\": [],\n    \"text_length\": 8387,\n    \"full_text\": \"South Brooklyn Health \\n5th Fl Dental Suite \\nBID SET \\nSECTION 024119 -\\nSELECTIVE DEMOLITION\\nCopyright \\u00a9 2024 by the American Institute of Architects. Warning: This AIA MasterSpec-based document is protected by U.S. Copyright Law and International\\nTreaties. It was created by \\\"Mancini Duffy\\\" for \\\"South Brooklyn Health 5th Fl Dental Suite\\\". A valid, current MasterSpec license is required for editing and use of this \\ndocument for any other project.(25268)\\n024119 - 1\\nSECTION 024119 - SELECTIVE DEMOLITION \\nPART 1 - GENERAL \\n1.1 \\nSUMMARY \\nA.\\nSection Includes:\\n1.\\nDemolition and removal of selected portions of building or structure.\\n2.\\nSalvage of existing items to be reused or recycled.\\n1.2 \\nMATERIALS OWNERSHIP \\nA.\\nUnless otherwise indicated, demolition waste becomes property of Contractor.\\n1.3\\nINFORMATIONAL SUBMITTALS \\n1.4\\nCLOSEOUT SUBMITTALS \\n1.5\\nQUALITY ASSURANCE \\n1.6\\nFIELD CONDITIONS \\nA.\\nOwner will occupy portions of building immediately adjacent to selective\\nConduct selective demolition so Owner's operations will not be disrupted.\\ndemolition area.\\nB.\\nNotify Architect and Owner of discrepancies between existing conditions and Drawings before\\nproceeding with selective demolition.\\nC.\\nStorage or sale of removed items or materials on-site is not permitted.\\nD.\\nArrange selective demolition schedule so as not to interfere with Owner's operations.\\nPART 2 - PRODUCTS \\n2.1 \\nPERFORMANCE REQUIREMENTS \\nA. \\nRegulatory Requirements: Comply with governing EPA notification regulations before beginning\\nselective demolition. Comply with hauling and disposal regulations of authorities having\\njurisdiction.\\nB. \\nStandards: Comply with ANSI/ASSP A10.6 and NFPA 241.\\n\\nSouth Brooklyn Health \\n5th Fl Dental Suite \\nBID SET \\nSECTION 024119 -\\nSELECTIVE DEMOLITION\\nCopyright \\u00a9 2024 by the American Institute of Architects. Warning: This AIA MasterSpec-based document is protected by U.S. Copyright Law and International\\nTreaties. It was created by \\\"Mancini Duffy\\\" for \\\"South Brooklyn Health 5th Fl Dental Suite\\\". A valid, current MasterSpec license is required for editing and use of this \\ndocument for any other project.(25268)\\n024119 - 2\\nPART 3 - EXECUTION \\n3.1 \\nEXAMINATION \\nA.\\nVerify that utilities have been disconnected and capped before starting selective demolition\\noperations.\\nB.\\nInventory and record the condition of items to be removed and salvaged.\\n3.2 \\nPREPARATION \\n3.3 \\nUTILITY SERVICES AND MECHANICAL/ELECTRICAL SYSTEMS \\nA.\\nExisting Services/Systems to Be Removed, Relocated, or Abandoned: Locate, identify,\\ndisconnect, and seal or cap off utility services and mechanical/electrical systems serving areas to\\nbe selectively demolished.\\n1.\\nArrange with Owner to shut off utilities with utility companies.\\n2.\\nIf services/systems are required to be removed, relocated, or abandoned, provide temporary\\nservices/systems that bypass area of selective demolition and that maintain continuity of\\nservices/systems to other parts of building.\\n3.\\nDisconnect, demolish, and remove fire-suppression systems, plumbing, and HVAC\\nsystems, equipment, and components indicated on Drawings to be removed.\\na.\\nPiping to Be Removed: Remove portion of piping indicated to be removed and cap\\nor plug remaining piping with same or compatible piping material.\\nb.\\nPiping to Be Abandoned in Place: Drain piping and cap or plug piping with same or\\ncompatible piping material and leave in place.\\nc.\\nEquipment to Be Removed: Disconnect and cap services and remove equipment.\\nd.\\nEquipment to Be Removed and Reinstalled: Disconnect and cap services and\\nremove, clean, and store equipment; when appropriate, reinstall, reconnect, and\\nmake equipment operational.\\ne.\\nEquipment to Be Removed and Salvaged: Disconnect and cap services and remove\\nequipment and deliver to Owner.\\nf.\\nDucts to Be Removed: Remove portion of ducts indicated to be removed and plug\\nremaining ducts with same or compatible ductwork material.\\ng.\\nDucts to Be Abandoned in Place: Cap or plug ducts with same or compatible\\nductwork material and leave in place.\\n3.4 \\nPROTECTION \\nA.\\nRemove temporary barricades and protections where hazards no longer exist.\\n\\nSouth Brooklyn Health \\n5th Fl Dental Suite \\nBID SET \\nSECTION 024119 -\\nSELECTIVE DEMOLITION\\nCopyright \\u00a9 2024 by the American Institute of Architects. Warning: This AIA MasterSpec-based document is protected by U.S. Copyright Law and International\\nTreaties. It was created by \\\"Mancini Duffy\\\" for \\\"South Brooklyn Health 5th Fl Dental Suite\\\". A valid, current MasterSpec license is required for editing and use of this \\ndocument for any other project.(25268)\\n024119 - 3\\n \\n \\n \\n3.5 \\nSELECTIVE DEMOLITION \\n \\nA. \\nGeneral: Demolish and remove existing construction only to the extent required by new \\nconstruction and as indicated. Use methods required to complete the Work within limitations of \\ngoverning regulations and as follows: \\n \\n1. \\nNeatly cut openings and holes plumb, square, and true to dimensions required. Use cutting \\nmethods least likely to damage construction to remain or adjoining construction. Use hand \\ntools or small power tools designed for sawing or grinding, not hammering and chopping. \\nTemporarily cover openings to remain. \\n2. \\nCut or drill from the exposed or finished side into concealed surfaces to avoid marring \\nexisting finished surfaces. \\n3. \\nDo not use cutting torches until work area is cleared of flammable materials. At concealed \\nspaces, such as duct and pipe interiors, verify condition and contents of hidden space before \\nstarting flame-cutting operations. Maintain portable fire-suppression devices during flame-\\ncutting operations. \\n4. \\nMaintain fire watch during and for as required hours after flame-cutting operations, if any. \\n5. \\nLocate selective demolition equipment and remove debris and materials so as not to impose \\nexcessive loads on supporting walls, floors, or framing. \\n6. \\nDispose of demolished items and materials promptly. Comply with requirements in Section \\n017419 \\\"Construction Waste Management and Disposal.\\\" \\n \\nB. \\nRemoved and Salvaged Items: \\n \\n1. \\nClean salvaged items. \\n2. \\nStore items in a secure area until delivery to Owner. \\n3. \\nProtect items from damage during transport and storage. \\n \\nC. \\nRemoved and Reinstalled Items: \\n \\n1. \\nClean and repair items to functional condition adequate for intended reuse. \\n2. \\nPack or crate items after cleaning and repairing. Identify contents of containers. \\n3. \\nProtect items from damage during transport and storage. \\n4. \\nReinstall items in locations indicated. Comply with installation requirements for new \\nmaterials and equipment. Provide connections, supports, and miscellaneous materials \\nnecessary to make item functional for use indicated. \\n \\nD. \\nExisting Items to Remain: Protect construction indicated to remain against damage and soiling \\nduring selective demolition. Clean and reinstall in their original locations after selective \\ndemolition operations are complete. \\n \\n3.6 \\nCLEANING \\n \\nA. \\nRemove demolition waste materials from Project site and dispose of them in an EPA-approved \\nconstruction and demolition waste landfill acceptable to authorities having jurisdiction. and \\nrecycle or dispose of them according to Section 017419 \\\"Construction Waste Management and \\nDisposal.\\\" \\n \\n1. \\nDo not allow demolished materials to accumulate on-site. \\n\\nSouth Brooklyn Health \\n5th Fl Dental Suite \\nBID SET \\nSECTION 024119 -\\nSELECTIVE DEMOLITION\\nCopyright \\u00a9 2024 by the American Institute of Architects. Warning: This AIA MasterSpec-based document is protected by U.S. Copyright Law and International\\nTreaties. It was created by \\\"Mancini Duffy\\\" for \\\"South Brooklyn Health 5th Fl Dental Suite\\\". A valid, current MasterSpec license is required for editing and use of this \\ndocument for any other project.(25268)\\n024119 - 4\\n2.\\nRemove and transport debris in a manner that will prevent spillage on adjacent surfaces\\nand areas.\\n3.\\nRemove debris from elevated portions of building by chute, hoist, or other device that will\\nconvey debris to grade level in a controlled descent. Coordinate with Building\\nMa\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"section\": \"061000\",\n    \"title\": \"ROUGH\",\n    \"division\": \"06\",\n    \"start_page\": 81,\n    \"manufacturers\": [],\n    \"products\": [\n      \"1.\",\n      \"Treated materials are to have a moisture content of 28 percent or less when\",\n      \"Install engineered wood products to comply with\"\n    ],\n    \"text_length\": 8113,\n    \"full_text\": \"South Brooklyn Health \\n5th Fl Dental Suite \\nBID SET \\nSECTION 061000 - ROUGH\\nCARPENTRY\\nCopyright \\u00a9 2024 by the American Institute of Architects. Warning: This AIA MasterSpec-based document is protected by U.S. Copyright Law and International\\nTreaties. It was created by \\\"Mancini Duffy\\\" for \\\"South Brooklyn Health 5th Fl Dental Suite\\\". A valid, current MasterSpec license is required for editing and use of this \\ndocument for any other project.(25268)\\n061000 - 1\\nSECTION 061000 - ROUGH CARPENTRY \\nPART 1 - GENERAL \\n1.1 \\nSUMMARY \\nA.\\nSection Includes:\\n1.\\nWood blocking and nailers.\\n2.\\nWood sleepers.\\n3.\\nPlywood backing panels.\\n1.2 \\nACTION SUBMITTALS \\nA.\\nProduct Data:\\n1.\\nFor each type of process and factory-fabricated product.\\n1.3 \\nINFORMATIONAL SUBMITTALS \\n1.4 \\nQUALITY ASSURANCE \\nPART 2 - PRODUCTS \\n2.1 \\nWOOD PRODUCTS, GENERAL \\nA. \\nLumber: Comply with DOC PS 20 and applicable rules of grading agencies indicated. If no\\ngrading agency is indicated, comply with the applicable rules of any rules-writing agency certified\\nby the ALSC Board of Review. Grade lumber by an agency certified by the ALSC Board of\\nReview to inspect and grade lumber under the rules indicated.\\n1.\\nFactory mark each piece of lumber with grade stamp of grading agency.\\n2.\\nFor exposed lumber indicated to receive a stained or natural finish, mark grade stamp on\\nend or back of each piece .\\n3.\\nDress lumber, S4S, unless otherwise indicated.\\nB. \\nMaximum Moisture Content:\\n1.\\nBoards: 19 percent unless otherwise indicated.\\n\\nSouth Brooklyn Health \\n5th Fl Dental Suite \\nBID SET \\nSECTION 061000 - ROUGH\\nCARPENTRY\\nCopyright \\u00a9 2024 by the American Institute of Architects. Warning: This AIA MasterSpec-based document is protected by U.S. Copyright Law and International\\nTreaties. It was created by \\\"Mancini Duffy\\\" for \\\"South Brooklyn Health 5th Fl Dental Suite\\\". A valid, current MasterSpec license is required for editing and use of this \\ndocument for any other project.(25268)\\n061000 - 2\\n2.2 \\nFIRE-RETARDANT-TREATMENT \\nA. \\nGeneral: Where fire-retardant-treated materials are indicated, materials are to comply with\\nrequirements in this article, that are acceptable to authorities having jurisdiction, and with fire- \\ntest-response characteristics specified as determined by testing identical products per test method\\nindicated by a qualified testing agency.\\nB. \\nFire-Retardant-Treated Lumber and Plywood by Pressure Process: Products with a flame-spread\\nindex of 25 or less when tested according to ASTM E84, and with no evidence of significant\\nprogressive combustion when the test is extended an additional 20 minutes, and with the flame\\nfront not extending more than 10.5 feet beyond the centerline of the burners at any time during\\nthe test.\\n1.\\nInterior Type A: Treated materials are to have a moisture content of 28 percent or less when\\ntested according to ASTM D3201/D3201M at 92 percent relative humidity. Use where\\nexterior type is not indicated.\\nC. \\nKiln-dry lumber after treatment to maximum moisture content of 19 percent. Kiln-dry plywood\\nafter treatment to maximum moisture content of 15 percent.\\nD. \\nIdentify fire-retardant-treated wood with appropriate classification marking of qualified testing\\nagency.\\nE. \\nE. Application: Treat all rough carpentry unless otherwise indicated\\n2.3 \\nPLYWOOD BACKING PANELS \\nA. \\nEquipment Backing Panels: Plywood, DOC PS 1, , fire-retardant treated, in thickness indicated\\nor, if not indicated, not less than 3/4-inch nominal thickness.\\n2.4 \\nFASTENERS \\nA. \\nGeneral: Fasteners are to be of size and type indicated and comply with requirements specified in\\nthis article for material and manufacture. Provide nails or screws, in sufficient length, to penetrate\\nnot less than 1-1/2 inches into wood substrate.\\n1.\\nWhere rough carpentry is exposed to weather, in ground contact, pressure-preservative\\ntreated, or in area of high relative humidity, provide fasteners with hot-dip zinc coating\\ncomplying with ASTM A153/A153M or of Type 304 stainless steel.\\nB. \\nPower-Driven Fasteners: Fastener systems with an evaluation report acceptable to authorities\\nhaving jurisdiction, based on ICC-ES AC70.\\n2.5 \\nMETAL FRAMING ANCHORS \\nA. \\nAllowable design loads, as published by manufacturer, are to meet or exceed those of products of\\nmanufacturers listed. Manufacturer's published values are to be determined from empirical data\\nor by rational engineering analysis and demonstrated by comprehensive testing performed\\n\\nSouth Brooklyn Health \\n5th Fl Dental Suite \\nBID SET \\nSECTION 061000 - ROUGH\\nCARPENTRY\\nCopyright \\u00a9 2024 by the American Institute of Architects. Warning: This AIA MasterSpec-based document is protected by U.S. Copyright Law and International\\nTreaties. It was created by \\\"Mancini Duffy\\\" for \\\"South Brooklyn Health 5th Fl Dental Suite\\\". A valid, current MasterSpec license is required for editing and use of this \\ndocument for any other project.(25268)\\n061000 - 3\\n \\n \\n \\nby a qualified independent testing agency. Framing anchors are to be punched for fasteners \\nadequate to withstand same loads as framing anchors. \\n \\nB. \\nGalvanized-Steel Sheet: Hot-dip, zinc-coated steel sheet complying with ASTM A653/A653M, \\nG60 coating designation. \\n \\n1. \\nUse for interior locations unless otherwise indicated. \\n \\nC. \\nHot-Dip, Heavy-Galvanized Steel Sheet: ASTM A653/A653M; structural steel (SS), high- \\nstrength low-alloy steel Type A (HSLAS Type A), or high-strength low-alloy steel Type B \\n(HSLAS Type B); G185 coating designation; and not less than 0.036 inch thick. \\n \\n1. \\nUse for wood-preservative-treated lumber and where indicated. \\n \\nPART 3 - EXECUTION \\n \\n3.1 \\nINSTALLATION \\n \\nA. \\nFraming Standard: Comply with AF&PA's WCD 1, \\\"Details for Conventional Wood Frame \\nConstruction,\\\" unless otherwise indicated. \\n \\nB. \\nFraming with Engineered Wood Products: Install engineered wood products to comply with \\nmanufacturer's written instructions. \\n \\nC. \\nSet work to required levels and lines, with members plumb, true to line, cut, and fitted. Fit rough \\ncarpentry accurately to other construction. Locate furring, nailers, blocking, and similar supports \\nto comply with requirements for attaching other construction. \\n \\nD. \\nInstall shear wall panels to comply with manufacturer's written instructions. \\n \\nE. \\nInstall metal framing anchors to comply with manufacturer's written instructions. Install fasteners \\nthrough each fastener hole. \\n \\nF. \\nDo not splice structural members between supports unless otherwise indicated. \\n \\nG. \\nComply with AWPA M4 for applying field treatment to cut surfaces of preservative-treated \\nlumber. \\n \\nH. \\nWhere wood-preservative-treated lumber is installed adjacent to metal decking, install continuous \\nflexible flashing separator between wood and metal decking. \\n \\nI. \\nSecurely attach rough carpentry work to substrate by anchoring and fastening as indicated, \\ncomplying with the following: \\n \\n1. \\nTable 2304.9.1, \\\"Fastening Schedule,\\\" in ICC's International Building Code (IBC). \\n2. \\nTable R602.3(1), \\\"Fastener Schedule for Structural Members,\\\" and Table R602.3(2), \\n\\\"Alternate Attachments,\\\" in ICC's International Residential Code for One- and Two- \\nFamily Dwellings. \\n3. \\nICC-ES evaluation report for fastener. \\n\\nSouth Brooklyn Health \\n5th Fl Dental Suite \\nBID SET \\nSECTION 061000 - ROUGH\\nCARPENTRY\\nCopyright \\u00a9 2024 by the American Institute of Architects. Warning: This AIA MasterSpec-based document is protected by U.S. Copyright Law and International\\nTreaties. It was created by \\\"Mancini Duffy\\\" for \\\"South Brooklyn Health 5th Fl Dental Suite\\\". A valid, current MasterSpec license is required for editing and use of this \\ndocument for any other project.(25268)\\n061000 - 4\\n3.2 \\nPROTECTION \\nA. \\nProtect wood that has been treated with inorganic boron (SBX) from weather. If, despite\\nprotection, inorganic boron-treated wood becomes wet, apply EPA-registered borate treatment.\\nApply borate solution by spraying to comply with EPA-registered label.\\nB. \\nProtect rough carpentry from weather. If, despite protection, rough carpentry becomes wet , apply\\nEPA-registered bo\"\n  }\n]","cwd":"/home/ec2-user/clawd"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1769807688542}}
{"type":"message","id":"a4a3e2cf","parentId":"2ae93567","timestamp":"2026-01-30T21:17:38.882Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the project state. Let me create the architecture document."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_0184zS6zjxVep367XT7PSjAm","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md","content":"# Team of Rivals — Multi-Agent Bid Takeoff Architecture\n## HHC South Brooklyn Health, Dental Suite 5th Floor | Project #26202103\n\n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Date:** 2025-07-11  \n**Reference:** arxiv.org/abs/2601.14351 (specialized agent teams with opposing incentives)\n\n---\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nWe have 298 bid line items, 86 drawing sheets, a 1,058-page spec, and most quantities still unmeasured. The single-agent approach can't hold enough context, can't self-check, and produces errors like the GWB layer discrepancy (Item 51) and the missing fire-rated door. This architecture splits the work across four specialized agents with adversarial incentives—each agent's success metric conflicts with at least one other agent's, ensuring errors get caught.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Agent Roles\n\n### 1A. PLANNER (Orchestrator)\n\n**Role:** Decomposes bid line items into discrete tasks, sequences work, manages dependencies, tracks progress.\n\n**Incentive:** Throughput — measured by items completed per cycle.\n\n**Responsibilities:**\n- Read `bid_breakdown.json` and `takeoff_checklist.md` to identify next batch of unmeasured items\n- Group items by drawing sheet (e.g., all items needing A-100.00 go together)\n- Create task packets for Executor with minimal, relevant context\n- Track completion status in `takeoff_progress.json`\n- Decide when to escalate to Doug vs. auto-resolve\n\n**Prompt Template:**\n```\nYou are the Planner for HHC Brooklyn Dental bid takeoff (Project #26202103).\n\nCONTEXT FILES (read these):\n- bid_breakdown.json (298 line items, current quantities)\n- takeoff_progress.json (completion status)\n- takeoff_checklist.md (known gaps and flags)\n\nYOUR JOB: Select the next batch of 5-10 bid line items to quantify. For each item, produce a TASK PACKET:\n\n{\n  \"item_number\": <int>,\n  \"description\": \"<from bid breakdown>\",\n  \"unit\": \"<SF/LF/EA/LS>\",\n  \"sheets_needed\": [\"<sheet IDs from drawing_index.json>\"],\n  \"spec_sections\": [\"<section numbers from specs_summary.json>\"],\n  \"context_summary\": \"<1-2 sentences of relevant info from scope_analysis.md>\",\n  \"known_flags\": [\"<any flags from takeoff_checklist.md>\"],\n  \"depends_on\": [<item numbers that must be done first, if any>]\n}\n\nRULES:\n- Group items that share the same drawing sheet\n- Prioritize Doug's direct scope (Demo, Finishes, Specialties, Openings, Carpentry)\n- Do NOT include raw spec text — summaries only\n- Flag items with known discrepancies for Critic review\n\nOutput: JSON array of task packets + updated priority queue.\n```\n\n### 1B. EXECUTOR (Quantity Takeoff Agent)\n\n**Role:** Reads drawing sheets, measures quantities, maps to bid line items.\n\n**Incentive:** Accuracy of measurements — penalized for quantities that Critic rejects.\n\n**Responsibilities:**\n- Receive task packet from Planner\n- Read specified drawing sheet images (PNG from `/sheets/`)\n- Extract quantities: count items (EA), measure linear footage (LF), calculate areas (SF)\n- Cross-reference room_schedule.json and finish_schedule.json for area validation\n- Produce structured quantity output with measurement methodology notes\n\n**Prompt Template:**\n```\nYou are the Executor for HHC Brooklyn Dental bid takeoff.\n\nTASK PACKET:\n{task_packet_json}\n\nREFERENCE DATA (loaded for you):\n- Drawing sheet image(s): {sheet_paths}\n- room_schedule.json (room names, areas, finishes)\n- finish_schedule.json (finish types by room)\n- partition_schedule.json (partition type assemblies)\n- door_schedule.json (door types, sizes, ratings)\n\nYOUR JOB: Measure the quantity for each bid item in the task packet.\n\nFor EACH item, output:\n{\n  \"item_number\": <int>,\n  \"quantity\": <number>,\n  \"unit\": \"<SF/LF/EA>\",\n  \"measurement_method\": \"<how you derived this — which rooms, what dimensions, what formula>\",\n  \"sheets_used\": [\"<which sheets you actually read>\"],\n  \"confidence\": \"high|medium|low\",\n  \"assumptions\": [\"<any assumptions made>\"],\n  \"cross_check\": \"<sanity check — e.g., 'total partition SF consistent with ~3,500 SF suite area'>\",\n  \"flags\": [\"<anything unexpected>\"]\n}\n\nRULES:\n- Show your math. Every quantity must have a derivation.\n- If a drawing is illegible or ambiguous, say so — don't guess.\n- Use room_schedule areas as sanity checks, NOT as primary measurements.\n- Flag any discrepancy between what you see on drawings and what the bid breakdown describes.\n- For partitions: measure EACH FACE separately (two finishes = two measurements).\n- For doors: note size, rating, and hardware set from schedule.\n```\n\n### 1C. CRITIC (Adversarial QA Agent)\n\n**Role:** Reviews Executor output for errors, omissions, inconsistencies. Actively tries to find problems.\n\n**Incentive:** Error detection rate — rewarded for finding real errors, penalized for false positives.\n\n**Responsibilities:**\n- Cross-reference quantities against specs, drawings, and bid breakdown\n- Check unit consistency (SF vs LF vs EA)\n- Verify code compliance (fire ratings, ADA, ICRA)\n- Look for missing items (things on drawings not in bid)\n- Challenge Executor's assumptions and math\n- Produce accept/reject/flag decisions\n\n**Prompt Template:**\n```\nYou are the Critic for HHC Brooklyn Dental bid takeoff. Your job is to FIND ERRORS.\n\nEXECUTOR OUTPUT:\n{executor_output_json}\n\nORIGINAL TASK PACKET:\n{task_packet_json}\n\nREFERENCE DATA:\n- specs_summary.json (spec requirements by section)\n- partition_schedule.json (assembly details — layers, ratings)\n- door_schedule.json (sizes, ratings, hardware sets)\n- scope_analysis.md § \"CRITICAL FLAGS SUMMARY\"\n- Known discrepancies from takeoff_checklist.md\n\nRUN THESE CHECKS (all of them, every time):\n\n1. UNIT CONSISTENCY\n   - Does the measured unit match the bid breakdown unit?\n   - Is SF used where LF should be (or vice versa)?\n   - Flag: Item 58 (epoxy floor) is LF — verify this is correct\n\n2. SPEC vs DRAWING CROSS-REFERENCE\n   - Does the spec require something not reflected in the quantity?\n   - Example: Spec 092900 requires Type X GWB for fire-rated assemblies — did Executor account for 2 layers?\n   - Example: Spec 093013 says \"no substitutions\" on tile — is the specified product noted?\n\n3. MISSING ITEMS\n   - Is there anything visible on the drawing that has NO corresponding bid line item?\n   - Example: Fire-rated door at Mechanical Room — is it in the bid?\n   - Example: Lead-lined GWB at Panorex room — is it in the bid?\n\n4. CODE COMPLIANCE\n   - Fire-rated partitions: correct UL assembly? Correct number of GWB layers?\n   - ADA: grab bars at all required locations? Clearances?\n   - ICRA: dust control and infection barriers accounted for?\n\n5. MATH VERIFICATION\n   - Recalculate at least 2 quantities independently\n   - Check that room areas sum to approximately suite total (~3,500 SF)\n   - Check that partition SF is reasonable for the suite size\n\n6. KNOWN ISSUES CHECK\n   - Item 51 GWB layer discrepancy — is this addressed?\n   - Fire-rated door missing from bid — is this flagged?\n   - Altro Whitrock vs ceramic tile overlap — is this clarified?\n\nOUTPUT for each item:\n{\n  \"item_number\": <int>,\n  \"verdict\": \"ACCEPT|REJECT|FLAG\",\n  \"issues\": [\n    {\n      \"severity\": \"CRITICAL|WARNING|INFO\",\n      \"check\": \"<which check caught this>\",\n      \"description\": \"<what's wrong>\",\n      \"evidence\": \"<spec section, drawing reference, or math>\",\n      \"recommendation\": \"<fix or escalate>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"revised_quantity\": <number or null if ACCEPT>,\n  \"escalate_to_doug\": <boolean>\n}\n```\n\n### 1D. DOMAIN EXPERT (Spec & Code Specialist)\n\n**Role:** Deep-dives into specific technical questions that Planner/Executor/Critic can't resolve. Called on-demand, not every cycle.\n\n**Incentive:** Resolution quality — measured by whether escalated issues get resolved without re-escalation.\n\n**Responsibilities:**\n- Answer specific technical questions about specs, codes, UL assemblies\n- Resolve ambiguities between drawings and specifications\n- Provide manufacturer/product-specific guidance\n- Draft RFI language when clarification needed from architect\n\n**Prompt Template:**\n```\nYou are a Domain Expert for HHC Brooklyn Dental bid takeoff. You specialize in {domain}.\n\nQUESTION:\n{specific_question}\n\nRELEVANT SPEC TEXT:\n{spec_excerpt — max 2000 chars}\n\nRELEVANT DRAWING DATA:\n{drawing_notes_or_schedule_excerpt}\n\nPROJECT CONTEXT:\n- Healthcare dental clinic renovation, 5th floor, occupied hospital\n- NYC building code applies\n- Architect: Mancini Duffy\n- Single prime contract\n\nProvide:\n1. ANSWER: Clear, definitive answer with code/spec citations\n2. CONFIDENCE: How certain are you? (high/medium/low)\n3. IF LOW CONFIDENCE: Draft RFI question for the architect\n4. PRICING IMPACT: Does this change the bid quantity or cost? How?\n```\n\n**Domain specializations (spawn the right one):**\n- `fire-protection`: UL assemblies, NFPA 80, fire ratings, firestopping\n- `finishes`: GWB types, tile, flooring, paint systems, healthcare finishes\n- `doors-hardware`: hollow metal, hardware sets, keying, access control\n- `healthcare-code`: ICRA, infection control, ADA, NYC DOB requirements\n- `dental-equipment`: Henry Schein coordination, dental air/vacuum, X-ray shielding\n\n---\n\n## 2. Workflow Pipeline\n\n```\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    PLANNER                                │\n│  Reads: bid_breakdown.json, takeoff_progress.json        │\n│  Outputs: Task Packets (batches of 5-10 items)           │\n└────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┘\n                     │ Task Packet\n                     ▼\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    EXECUTOR                               │\n│  Reads: Drawing PNGs, room/finish/partition/door JSONs   │\n│  Outputs: Quantities + measurement methodology           │\n└────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┘\n                     │ Executor Output\n                     ▼\n┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                    CRITIC                                 │\n│  Reads: Executor output + specs + known flags            │\n│  Outputs: ACCEPT / REJECT / FLAG per item                │\n└──────┬─────────────┬──────────────────┬─────────────────┘\n       │             │                  │\n  ACCEPT         REJECT              FLAG\n       │             │                  │\n       ▼             ▼                  ▼\n  ┌─────────┐  ┌──────────┐    ┌──────────────┐\n  │ Commit   │  │ Back to  │    │ DOMAIN       │\n  │ to JSON  │  │ Executor │    │ EXPERT       │\n  │          │  │ (retry)  │    │ (on-demand)  │\n  └─────────┘  └──────────┘    └──────┬───────┘\n                                      │\n                               ┌──────┴───────┐\n                               │  Resolved?   │\n                               ├──Yes──┐      │\n                               │       ▼      │No\n                               │   Commit     │\n                               │              ▼\n                               │     ESCALATE TO DOUG\n                               └──────────────┘\n```\n\n### Step-by-Step for a Single Bid Line Item\n\n**Example: Item 50 — W32 partition, 3-5/8\" studs, 1×5/8\" GWB ea side, non-rated**\n\n1. **Planner** creates task packet:\n   - Sheets needed: A-100.00 (construction plan, part3/p12)\n   - Spec sections: 092216 (metal framing), 092900 (GWB)\n   - Context: \"Non-rated partition type W3. Measure total LF from construction plan, multiply by wall height for SF. Note: W2 is the 1-HR rated variant — don't confuse them.\"\n   - Known flags: \"Item 51 has GWB layer discrepancy for W2 type\"\n\n2. **Executor** reads sheet part3/page-12 (A-100.00):\n   - Identifies all W3-type partition segments on plan\n   - Measures linear footage of each segment\n   - Multiplies by ceiling height (likely 9'-0\" or per partition schedule)\n   - Calculates SF for both faces\n   - Output: `{ quantity: 1240, unit: \"SF\", method: \"7 segments totaling 138 LF × 9' height = 1,242 SF, rounded to 1,240\", confidence: \"medium\" }`\n\n3. **Critic** reviews:\n   - ✅ Unit matches bid (SF)\n   - ✅ Math checks out (138 × 9 ≈ 1,242)\n   - ⚠️ WARNING: \"Executor measured total SF but bid may want per-face SF. Clarify: is 1,240 SF one face or both?\"\n   - ✅ No spec conflicts for non-rated assembly\n   - Verdict: FLAG — needs clarification on face count\n\n4. **Domain Expert** (finishes) resolves:\n   - \"Industry standard for partition SF in bid breakdowns: measure one face. The bid line item covers the assembly, not individual faces. 1,240 SF = one face is correct if that's the total surface area of one side.\"\n   - Resolved → Commit\n\n5. **Planner** updates `takeoff_progress.json`:\n   - Item 50: `{ status: \"complete\", quantity: 1240, unit: \"SF\", reviewed: true }`\n\n---\n\n## 3. Adversarial Checks (Critic Agent)\n\n### Check Matrix\n\n| # | Check Name | What It Catches | Example from This Project |\n|---|-----------|-----------------|--------------------------|\n| 1 | **Unit Mismatch** | Wrong measurement unit | Item 58: epoxy floor in LF (should be SF?) |\n| 2 | **Layer Count** | Missing GWB layers in rated assemblies | Item 51: bid says 1 layer, UL requires 2 for 1-HR |\n| 3 | **Missing Scope** | Items on drawings not in bid | Fire-rated door at Mech Room (Door 1 = ¾-HR) |\n| 4 | **Spec Conflict** | Drawing ≠ spec requirement | No-sub clause on tile (093013) — must price exact product |\n| 5 | **Double Count** | Same work counted in two items | Rough carpentry blocking vs. wall protection backing |\n| 6 | **Area Sanity** | Total areas exceed suite footprint | Sum of all floor finishes > ~3,500 SF = problem |\n| 7 | **Code Compliance** | Missing code-required items | Lead-lined GWB at Panorex X-ray room |\n| 8 | **Orphan Items** | Bid items with no drawing reference | Items that can't be traced to any sheet |\n| 9 | **Price Reasonableness** | Quantities that imply absurd costs | 10,000 SF of partition in a 3,500 SF suite |\n| 10 | **Coordination Gap** | MEP/arch conflicts | Dental chair rough-in vs. Henry Schein coordination |\n\n### Cross-Reference Matrix (Critic must verify these intersections)\n\n```\n                    Drawings  Specs   Bid Breakdown  Schedules\nDrawings              —        ✓          ✓             ✓\nSpecs                 ✓        —          ✓             ✓\nBid Breakdown         ✓        ✓          —             ✓\nSchedules             ✓        ✓          ✓             —\n```\n\nFor every item, Critic checks at minimum 2 of 4 sources agree on: quantity unit, scope inclusion, and material specification.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Error Interception Rules\n\n### Decision Tree\n\n```\nERROR DETECTED\n    │\n    ├── Is it a UNIT MISMATCH (SF/LF/EA)?\n    │     └── AUTO-RESOLVE if obvious (e.g., \"LF\" for base = correct)\n    │         FLAG if ambiguous (e.g., Item 58 epoxy \"LF\" — could be either)\n    │\n    ├── Is it a MATH ERROR (wrong multiplication/addition)?\n    │     └── AUTO-RESOLVE: Critic recalculates, Executor retries\n    │\n    ├── Is it a MISSING ITEM (on drawing, not in bid)?\n    │     └── ESCALATE TO DOUG — he decides if it's an RFI or an add\n    │\n    ├── Is it a SPEC vs DRAWING CONFLICT?\n    │     ├── Minor (paint sheen, sealant type): FLAG, note in output\n    │     └── Major (fire rating, structural): ESCALATE TO DOUG\n    │\n    ├── Is it a CODE COMPLIANCE issue?\n    │     └── ALWAYS ESCALATE TO DOUG\n    │\n    └── Is the Executor CONFIDENCE \"low\"?\n          └── DOMAIN EXPERT review before committing\n```\n\n### Severity Definitions\n\n| Level | Definition | Action | Example |\n|-------|-----------|--------|---------|\n| **CRITICAL** | Wrong quantity could lose the bid or create liability | STOP. Escalate to Doug immediately. | Fire-rated door missing, GWB layer discrepancy |\n| **WARNING** | Likely error but bounded financial impact | Domain Expert review, then commit or escalate | Unit ambiguity on rubber base (EA vs LF) |\n| **INFO** | Notable but not blocking | Log it, continue | Paint color code confirmation |\n\n### Auto-Resolve Conditions (no human needed)\n\n1. Math error where Critic's recalculation matches a known reference (room_schedule area)\n2. Obvious unit mapping (e.g., doors = EA, paint = SF, base = LF)\n3. Duplicate detection where one item clearly supersedes another\n4. Rounding differences < 5%\n\n### Escalate-to-Doug Triggers\n\n1. Any CRITICAL severity finding\n2. Spec vs drawing conflict on fire-rated assemblies\n3. Missing bid line item for scope visible on drawings\n4. Healthcare code compliance question (ICRA, X-ray shielding, ADA)\n5. Quantity confidence \"low\" after Domain Expert review\n6. Any item where financial impact > $5,000 estimated\n\n---\n\n## 5. Implementation Plan\n\n### 5A. File Structure\n\n```\nhhc-brooklyn-dental/\n├── TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md          ← this file\n├── takeoff_progress.json            ← master tracking (Planner updates)\n├── bid_breakdown.json               ← 298 items (source of truth)\n├── scope_analysis.md                ← project context summary\n├── takeoff_checklist.md             ← gap analysis\n├── agents/\n│   ├── planner_output/              ← task packets per batch\n│   │   ├── batch-001.json\n│   │   └── batch-002.json\n│   ├── executor_output/             ← quantity results per batch\n│   │   ├── batch-001-quantities.json\n│   │   └── batch-002-quantities.json\n│   ├── critic_output/               ← QA verdicts per batch\n│   │   ├── batch-001-review.json\n│   │   └── batch-002-review.json\n│   └── expert_output/               ← domain expert consultations\n│       ├── fire-rating-item51.json\n│       └── missing-door-item44.json\n├── escalations/                     ← items needing Doug's input\n│   ├── gwb-layer-discrepancy.md\n│   └── fire-rated-door-missing.md\n└── final/\n    └── completed_takeoff.json       ← final quantities, QA-passed\n```\n\n### 5B. Session Spawn Commands\n\nEach agent runs as a spawned subagent session. The main session (or a cron trigger) acts as the Planner, spawning Executor and Critic in sequence.\n\n#### Spawn Planner (or run in main session)\n```\nTask: Run the Planner role for HHC Brooklyn Dental takeoff.\n\nRead these files:\n- /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/bid_breakdown.json\n- /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/takeoff_progress.json\n- /home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/blueprint/hhc-brooklyn-dental/takeoff_checklist.md\n\nSelect the next batch of 5-10 unmeasured bid items from Doug's direct scope.\nGroup by drawing sheet. Output task packets to agents/planner_output/batch-{NNN}.json.\nUpdate takeoff_progress.json with status \"in-progress\" for selected items.\n```\n\n#### Spawn Executor\n```\nTask: Execute quantity takeoff for HHC Brooklyn Dental.\n\nRead your task packet: agents/planner_output/batch-{NNN}.json\n\nFor each item in the packet:\n1. Read the specified drawing sheet image(s) from /sheets/\n2. Read reference data: room_schedule.json, finish_schedule.json, partition_schedule.json, door_schedule.json\n3. Measure quantities per the Executor prompt template in TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md § 1B\n4. Write results to agents/executor_output/batch-{NNN}-quantities.json\n\nShow your work. Every number needs a derivation.\n```\n\n#### Spawn Critic\n```\nTask: QA review of quantity takeoff for HHC Brooklyn Dental.\n\nRead:\n- Executor output: agents/executor_output/batch-{NNN}-quantities.json\n- Original task packet: agents/planner_output/batch-{NNN}.json\n- specs_summary.json, partition_schedule.json, door_schedule.json\n- scope_analysis.md (Critical Flags section)\n- takeoff_checklist.md (Known Discrepancies section)\n\nRun ALL checks from TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md § 1C and § 3.\nOutput verdicts to agents/critic_output/batch-{NNN}-review.json.\n\nYour job is to find errors. Be adversarial. Challenge every assumption.\nIf something seems too convenient or too round, dig deeper.\n```\n\n#### Spawn Domain Expert (on-demand)\n```\nTask: Resolve technical question for HHC Brooklyn Dental takeoff.\n\nDomain: {fire-protection|finishes|doors-hardware|healthcare-code|dental-equipment}\n\nQuestion: {specific_question}\n\nRead relevant spec section from specs_summary.json (section {NNN}).\nRead relevant schedule from {schedule_file}.json.\n\nFollow the Domain Expert prompt template in TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md § 1D.\nWrite resolution to agents/expert_output/{topic-slug}.json.\n```\n\n### 5C. Orchestration Flow (Main Session Script)\n\n```\nBATCH_CYCLE:\n1. Run Planner → produces batch-{N}.json\n2. Spawn Executor subagent with batch-{N}\n3. Wait for Executor completion\n4. Spawn Critic subagent with batch-{N} results\n5. Wait for Critic completion\n6. Parse Critic output:\n   - ACCEPT items → write to final/completed_takeoff.json\n   - REJECT items → re-queue for Executor (max 2 retries)\n   - FLAG items → spawn Domain Expert OR write to escalations/\n7. Update takeoff_progress.json\n8. If items remain → goto BATCH_CYCLE\n9. When all items complete → generate final summary for Doug\n```\n\n### 5D. Progress Tracking Schema\n\n`takeoff_progress.json`:\n```json\n{\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"total_items\": 298,\n    \"dougs_scope_items\": 82,\n    \"last_updated\": \"2025-07-11T00:00:00Z\"\n  },\n  \"items\": {\n    \"50\": {\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"quantity\": 1240,\n      \"unit\": \"SF\",\n      \"batch\": \"batch-003\",\n      \"executor_confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"critic_verdict\": \"ACCEPT\",\n      \"retries\": 0,\n      \"completed_at\": \"2025-07-11T12:00:00Z\"\n    },\n    \"51\": {\n      \"status\": \"escalated\",\n      \"batch\": \"batch-003\",\n      \"escalation_reason\": \"GWB layer count discrepancy — bid says 1 layer, UL U419 requires 2\",\n      \"escalation_file\": \"escalations/gwb-layer-discrepancy.md\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"summary\": {\n    \"complete\": 0,\n    \"in_progress\": 0,\n    \"escalated\": 2,\n    \"not_started\": 296\n  }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Data Hygiene\n\n### The Problem\n\nThe spec PDF is 1,058 pages. The drawings are 86 sheets. Dumping raw content into agent context will:\n- Blow token limits\n- Dilute signal with noise\n- Cause hallucinations from irrelevant text\n\n### Rules\n\n| Rule | Implementation |\n|------|---------------|\n| **No raw PDF text in agent context** | Agents read `specs_summary.json` (pre-extracted, 21 sections with metadata). Full text only via Domain Expert on specific sections. |\n| **No full drawing sets** | Executor gets only the 1-3 sheet PNGs specified in the task packet. Never all 86. |\n| **Summaries over source** | Planner provides 1-2 sentence context summaries per item, not full spec paragraphs. |\n| **Structured data only** | All inter-agent communication via JSON files in `agents/` directory. No prose dumps. |\n| **Context ceiling: 8K tokens per agent** | Task packets, reference data, and instructions must fit in ~8K tokens. If they don't, split the batch. |\n| **Immutable source files** | `bid_breakdown.json`, `specs_summary.json`, schedules — agents READ these, never WRITE. Only `takeoff_progress.json` and `agents/` output files are writable. |\n\n### Pre-Extracted Reference Data (already exists)\n\n| File | Contents | Size | Agent Access |\n|------|----------|------|-------------|\n| `bid_breakdown.json` | 298 line items (no quantities yet) | Small | Planner |\n| `specs_summary.json` | 21 spec sections with titles, page ranges, manufacturers | Medium | Critic, Expert |\n| `room_schedule.json` | 30 rooms with areas and finish codes | Small | Executor |\n| `finish_schedule.json` | Finish types mapped to rooms | Small | Executor |\n| `partition_schedule.json` | Partition assembly details (W1-W4, F1, C00) | Small | Executor, Critic |\n| `door_schedule.json` | 25 doors with sizes, ratings, hardware | Small | Executor, Critic |\n| `drawing_index.json` | Sheet catalog with page references | Small | Planner |\n| `sheet_index.json` | 80 sheets with text previews | Medium | Planner |\n| `scope_analysis.md` | Full project analysis with flags | Large | Planner (full), others (flags section only) |\n\n### What Needs Creating\n\n| File | Purpose | Created By |\n|------|---------|-----------|\n| `takeoff_progress.json` | Master status tracker | Planner (initialize from bid_breakdown) |\n| `agents/` directory tree | Inter-agent communication | First Planner run |\n| `escalations/` directory | Doug review queue | Critic/Expert when escalating |\n| `final/completed_takeoff.json` | Accepted quantities | Orchestrator after Critic ACCEPT |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Known Issues to Pre-Seed\n\nThese issues from `takeoff_checklist.md` should be immediately routed to the appropriate agent on first run:\n\n| Issue | Route To | Priority |\n|-------|----------|----------|\n| Item 51 — GWB 1-HR partition layer count | Domain Expert (fire-protection) → Escalate to Doug | 🔴 CRITICAL |\n| Fire-rated door missing from bid (Door 1 = ¾-HR) | Critic → Escalate to Doug | 🔴 CRITICAL |\n| Item 58 — Epoxy floor unit is LF not SF | Critic → Domain Expert (finishes) | 🟡 WARNING |\n| Item 61 — Rubber base unit is EA | Critic → Domain Expert (finishes) | 🟡 WARNING |\n| Lead-lined GWB at Panorex room | Domain Expert (healthcare-code) | 🟡 WARNING |\n| Altro Whitrock vs ceramic tile scope overlap | Domain Expert (finishes) | 🟡 WARNING |\n| Henry Schein dental equipment coordination | Domain Expert (dental-equipment) → Escalate to Doug | 🟡 WARNING |\n| Hazmat/ACM abatement scope unknown (Item 7) | Escalate to Doug directly | 🔴 CRITICAL |\n\n---\n\n## 8. Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target | Measurement |\n|--------|--------|-------------|\n| Items completed per batch cycle | 5-10 | `takeoff_progress.json` |\n| Critic rejection rate | < 30% (improving over time) | Reject count / total reviewed |\n| False positive rate (Critic flags that aren't real) | < 15% | Doug overrides / total flags |\n| Time to complete Doug's scope (82 items) | 4-6 batch cycles | Progress tracking |\n| Escalations to Doug | < 10 total | Escalation directory count |\n| Zero missed scope items | 0 items on drawings not in bid (after full run) | Final audit |\n\n---\n\n## Appendix: Adversarial Incentive Structure\n\nPer arxiv.org/abs/2601.14351, opposing incentives prevent groupthink:\n\n| Agent | Wants | Opposes |\n|-------|-------|---------|\n| **Planner** | High throughput (more items done) | Critic (slows pipeline with rejections) |\n| **Executor** | High acceptance rate | Critic (rejects sloppy work) |\n| **Critic** | High error detection | Executor (wants clean pass), Planner (wants speed) |\n| **Domain Expert** | Definitive resolution | Critic (may re-flag), Planner (slows queue) |\n\nNo single agent can declare \"done\" — the Critic must approve, and the Critic is incentivized to find problems. The Planner is incentivized to move fast, creating natural tension that surfaces issues early.\n\n---\n\n*This architecture replaces the single-agent takeoff approach. First batch cycle should target Items 8-23 (Demolition) as these are prerequisite to all other quantities.*\n"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"anthropic","model":"claude-opus-4-5","usage":{"input":1,"output":8015,"cacheRead":12236,"cacheWrite":16900,"totalTokens":37152,"cost":{"input":0.000005,"output":0.200375,"cacheRead":0.006117999999999999,"cacheWrite":0.10562500000000001,"total":0.31212300000000004}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1769807688544}}
