{"type":"message","id":"aafe643a","parentId":"187b9800","timestamp":"2026-01-30T21:17:49.533Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Created `TAKEOFF-ARCHITECTURE.md` with the full multi-agent architecture.\n\n**What I built:**\n\n1. **4 Agent Roles** — Planner, Executor, Critic, Domain Expert — each with exact prompt templates, incentives, and responsibilities\n2. **Workflow Pipeline** — Visual flow diagram + step-by-step example using Item 50 (W32 partition) showing how a line item flows from task packet → measurement → QA → commit\n3. **10 Adversarial Checks** — Unit mismatch, layer count, missing scope, spec conflicts, double count, area sanity, code compliance, orphan items, price reasonableness, coordination gaps\n4. **Error Decision Tree** — Auto-resolve (math errors, obvious units), FLAG (ambiguous), ESCALATE to Doug (fire ratings, missing items, code issues, >$5K impact)\n5. **Implementation Plan** — File structure, spawn command templates for each agent, orchestration loop, progress tracking JSON schema\n6. **Data Hygiene** — 8K token ceiling per agent, summaries-only policy, structured JSON communication, immutable source files\n\n**Key design decisions:**\n- Pre-seeded the 8 known issues (GWB layers, missing fire door, etc.) with routing paths\n- First batch targets Demo items (8-23) since they're prerequisite to everything else\n- Adversarial incentive structure per the arxiv paper — Critic is rewarded for finding errors, Executor is penalized for rejections, creating natural tension\n- All existing JSON reference files (`room_schedule`, `partition_schedule`, etc.) are mapped to specific agent access patterns"}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"anthropic","model":"claude-opus-4-5","usage":{"input":1,"output":378,"cacheRead":29136,"cacheWrite":8066,"totalTokens":37581,"cost":{"input":0.000005,"output":0.00945,"cacheRead":0.014568,"cacheWrite":0.0504125,"total":0.0744355}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1769807858893